WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.800
AD CS matters twice in a migration.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:08.035
It issues the certificates devices, domain
controllers and smart cards depend on,

3
00:00:08.035 --> 00:00:10.960
and its misconfigurations
are among the most

4
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.962
direct privilege-escalation
paths in a forest.

5
00:00:13.962 --> 00:00:18.209
The ESC naming comes from
SpecterOps' 2021 research,

6
00:00:18.209 --> 00:00:24.725
which defined ESC1 through ESC8; its
tooling now covers patterns through ESC16.

7
00:00:24.725 --> 00:00:28.285
Defender for Identity's definitions
are the program's reference,

8
00:00:28.285 --> 00:00:30.725
and the assessment checks
the ones it can see.

9
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:34.537
ESC1 is a template where the
enrollee supplies the subject,

10
00:00:34.537 --> 00:00:39.049
an authentication extended key usage
is allowed, no manager approval

11
00:00:39.049 --> 00:00:43.250
or authorized signature is required,
and broad groups can enroll.

12
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.600
ESC2 is a template with the Any
Purpose usage, or none at all.

13
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.653
ESC4 is a template ACL that gives a broad

14
00:00:50.653 --> 00:00:54.262
group rights such as Full
Control or Write DACL.

15
00:00:54.262 --> 00:01:00.185
ESC6 is the CA flag that lets every requester
specify the subject alternative name

16
00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:05.763
for every template; Microsoft's fix is to
clear the flag and restart the service.

17
00:01:05.763 --> 00:01:10.638
ESC7 is broad groups holding
Manage CA or Manage Certificates.

18
00:01:10.638 --> 00:01:15.287
That one needs the CA's own security
settings, which Appendix B's collector

19
00:01:15.287 --> 00:01:20.013
doesn't read; Defender for Identity
assesses it where a sensor runs on the CA.

20
00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:22.180
The correction that matters most:

21
00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.438
the source's ESC1 check tested for
the Digital Signature key usage.

22
00:01:26.438 --> 00:01:30.351
Almost every template has that,
so it flagged nearly everything.

23
00:01:30.351 --> 00:01:35.425
The check has to test the extended key
usage for an authentication purpose: client

24
00:01:35.425 --> 00:01:40.350
authentication, smart card logon, PKINIT
client authentication, or Any Purpose.

25
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.461
And a template that passes
is a candidate only if a

26
00:01:43.461 --> 00:01:46.800
CA actually publishes it
and broad groups can enroll.

27
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.800
Report only.

28
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.497
Remediation belongs to the PKI owner,

29
00:01:50.497 --> 00:01:54.412
through the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 07.

30
00:01:54.412 --> 00:01:57.841
The same inventory also tells
you whether user certificates

31
00:01:57.841 --> 00:02:00.860
can move to Entra
certificate-based authentication.
